   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p1n3/2B1P3/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR w KQkq - 4 4",
   "line": [
    "Ne5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The early queen sortie is the amateur try — and rather than the automatic developing move, there's a way to maximise the advantage: the knight jumps to e5. It isn't quite a fork, since the bishop can escape, but it grabs the bishop pair early and drives the queen onto a horribly exposed square.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 1529,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/2n1P3/1Q6/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "Qb3",
    "Nxc4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 1530.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/2Q1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "Qxc4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 1546,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/4p3/2p5/2Q1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K1NR w KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "e6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now expand and put that uncontested light-squared bishop to work. Prefer e6 over the natural pawn move for two reasons: it readies a central break — one you won't rush, since opening the centre just for its own sake is unwise — and the bishop is destined for the fianchetto anyway, so it won't get trapped behind the pawns.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 1548.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/4p3/2p5/2Q1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 1589.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/4pn2/2p5/2Q1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 2 7",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 1592.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/4pn2/2p1P3/2Q5/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 1608.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp3ppp/4pn2/2ppP3/2Q5/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq d6 0 8",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Against the central push there's a nifty device to blunt the opponent's dark-square grip — the simple central knight move is fine, but the pawn to d5 is stronger, and if a queen check comes it can be met by a block or by dropping the knight home to d7.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 1619.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp3ppp/4pn2/1QppP3/8/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 1 8",
   "line": [
    "Qb5+"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 1628.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1n1ppp/4p3/1QppP3/8/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 2 9",
   "line": [
    "Nd7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 1636.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1n1ppp/4p3/1QppP3/8/3P1N2/PPP2PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "d3"
   ],
   "spoken": "No reason to trade queens with such a lead in the centre and in development. And when the queen's escape route gets closed off, file it away — start hunting for ways to exploit its vulnerability for queenside gains, and, if the opponent is careless, to trap it outright.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 1680,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/1p1n1ppp/p3p3/1QppP3/8/3P1N2/PPP2PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "a6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Turn the queen's awkwardness into space — a rook-pawn nudge to a6 chases it, and then b5 does double duty: it builds an imposing pawn mass and prepares the fianchetto onto a gorgeous long diagonal.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 1682.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/3n1ppp/p3p3/1pppP3/8/1Q1P1N2/PPP2PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Qb3",
    "b5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 1687,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/3n1ppp/p3p3/1pppP3/2P5/1Q1P1N2/PP3PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "c4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 1734.5,
   "fen": "r2qkb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/1pppP3/2P5/1Q1P1N2/PP3PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 1 12",
   "line": [
    "Bb7"
   ],
   "spoken": "When the opponent pushes back on the queenside, the sharpest reply is the immediate fianchetto — and it isn't even a sacrifice. Playing Benko-style, if the b-pawn is grabbed the bishop swings to a6 to press the weak d3-pawn, and without calculating a step further you can trust that there's full compensation, most likely more.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 1739.5,
   "fen": "r2qkb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/1PppP3/8/1Q1P1N2/PP3PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "cxb5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 1774,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/qPppP3/8/1Q1P1N2/PP3PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 1 13",
   "line": [
    "Qa5+"
   ],
   "spoken": "It's not even necessary to go that route — a queen check on a5 forks the king and the loose pawn. Turning the opponent's own aggression into a tactic is the cleanest path.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 1778.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/qPppP3/8/1Q1P1N2/PP1B1PPP/RN2K2R b KQkq - 2 13",
   "line": [
    "Bd2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 1780,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/qPppP3/8/1Q1P1N2/PP3PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 1 13",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "One line to calculate: if the knight interposes on c3, you must spot the pawn thrust to d4 winning it. A blocking piece pinned in front of the king is only as safe as the pawn break it invites.",
   "kind": "tactic",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 1784,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/1qppP3/8/1Q1P1N2/PP1B1PPP/RN2K2R w KQkq - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "Bd2",
    "Qxb5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now trading queens is suddenly good — the circumstances have flipped. Pieces are developed, and the opponent is left with two juicy targets, on d3 and on e5, both of which the position is built to hammer.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 1785.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/1b1n1ppp/p3p3/1QppP3/8/3P1N2/PP1B1PPP/RN2K2R b KQkq - 0 14",
